time limit. The presence of a time limit is the clearest admission that it’s not absolutely waterproof; it’s **water-resistant for a defined period under defined conditions**. The label “waterproof” on the box is a simplified promise that only holds true within that exact defined box. Once the time runs out (or depth exceeds, or water chemistry changes, or the phone is a year old), the “proof” is no longer binding. The next time you see “waterproof” on a phone, you can mentally translate it to: *“This phone is tested to survive the kind of water exposure a normal clumsy human might realistically encounter, and we’re confident it’ll stay dry for at least 30 minutes at the stated depth. After that, you’re on your own.”* It’s a nuanced promise dressed in absolute language.
